{{- /*
Context polix injects on every session start of a valid polix.yaml, agent neutral, as the agent reads it: the header
lines, then the "- " items on staging and commits, the root and the folders of the AI, with no blank lines; the
whole text is trimmed. Each line is concise and at most 120 characters with the default folders and a short root (a
test checks it): split a rule into several items rather than wrap it. The rules on git allow every form polix
recognises and name only what it misses; TestStartRulesForms of internal/app/hook/answer pins them.
Data: .Root (the root of the working tree, with forward slashes) and .Path (the folder of the AI, path of
polix.yaml cleaned, relative to the root, without a trailing slash; ai by default).
*/ -}}
This project is managed by polix.
Follow these rules:
- Stage files with git add <paths> or git stage: never git commit -a, -am, --all or paths after git commit.
- Run git commit by its name: never through a git alias, a variable, a script or a wrapper such as timeout.
- End the line with the git commit, or follow it only with &&: after ; or | polix cannot tell whether it succeeded.
- Pass the commit message with -m, -F or a heredoc inside -m, so git never opens an editor.
- Otherwise polix misses the staging or the commit and skips its gates.
- The root of the project is {{.Root}}.
- Use these folders, below the root, and no other paths:
  - {{.Path}}/rules/: the rules on how to write the code, one yaml file per topic.
  - {{.Path}}/plans/: the implementation plans, one per file.
  - {{.Path}}/reports/: the reports of audits and reviews, one per file.
  - {{.Path}}/tasks/: the backlog of tasks, one per file.
